### Exemplary Patent-Style Description

A printable paper article comprising a fibrous substrate having first and second opposed major surfaces, the fibrous substrate comprising cellulosic fibers at a fiber basis weight of about 35 g/m² to about 90 g/m² and an inorganic particulate filler in an amount of about 8 wt.% to about 30 wt.%, based on the total dry weight of the article. The inorganic particulate filler may comprise calcium carbonate, kaolin clay, talc, titanium dioxide, or combinations thereof.

The article further comprises a starch sizing composition disposed within, or on at least one surface of, the fibrous substrate in an amount of about 0.5 g/m² to about 6 g/m², dry basis. The starch sizing composition may comprise oxidized starch, cationic starch, hydroxyethylated starch, or combinations thereof. A multivalent metal salt is incorporated into or applied with the starch sizing composition in an amount of about 0.05 g/m² to about 2.0 g/m², dry salt basis. Suitable salts include calcium chloride, magnesium chloride, calcium nitrate, magnesium sulfate, aluminum sulfate, polyaluminum chloride, and combinations thereof.

The filler, starch sizing composition, and multivalent metal salt are selected and proportioned such that the article exhibits:

- an ISO opacity of at least about 88%, preferably at least about 92%;
- reduced printed-image showthrough relative to an otherwise identical paper lacking the multivalent metal salt;
- a machine-direction tensile index of at least about 30 N·m/g;
- a cross-machine-direction tensile index of at least about 15 N·m/g;
- a tear index of at least about 4 mN·m²/g in at least one principal direction; and
- an internal bond strength of at least about 100 J/m².

The multivalent metal salt may immobilize one or more ink colorants at or near the printed surface, thereby reducing ink penetration through the fibrous substrate and reducing visual showthrough without requiring a corresponding increase in basis weight. The starch sizing composition maintains surface integrity, print definition, and mechanical strength despite the relatively high filler content.

### Exemplary Method of Preparation

A method of preparing the printable paper article comprises:

1. forming an aqueous furnish comprising cellulosic fibers and about 8 wt.% to about 30 wt.% inorganic particulate filler;
2. depositing the furnish onto a forming fabric to produce a wet web;
3. dewatering and pressing the wet web;
4. drying the web to form a paper substrate having a fiber basis weight of about 35 g/m² to about 90 g/m²;
5. applying to at least one surface of the substrate an aqueous sizing formulation comprising starch and a dissolved multivalent metal salt;
6. controlling application such that the dried substrate contains about 0.5 g/m² to about 6 g/m² starch and about 0.05 g/m² to about 2.0 g/m² multivalent metal salt;
7. drying the treated substrate; and
8. optionally calendering the substrate to obtain a printable surface while retaining the specified opacity and mechanical-strength properties.

The starch and multivalent metal salt may be applied simultaneously from a size-press formulation or sequentially in separate treatment operations. The resulting article is suitable for inkjet, electrophotographic, flexographic, offset, or other printing processes and provides high opacity, low image showthrough, and sufficient strength for converting and duplex printing.
